@*
    Which change to look at: a short list of ranges — committed, last commit,
    uncommitted — and under a divider, the individual commits.

    Presentational, like everything in this folder. No IGitService, no
    System.IO, no LibGit2Sharp: the host hands over the ranges and the commits
    already computed, and this draws them and reports which one was chosen.
    Read-only in the same sense FolderView is read-only — nothing here checks
    anything out, and an affordance that looked like it might would be a lie.

    A listbox, and the alternatives were weighed:

      * A plain list of buttons. Eight tab stops between this and the file list
        beside it, and no way to say which one is *in effect* — `aria-current`
        means "current within a set of links", which this is not.
      * A `radiogroup`. Semantically defensible, but ARIA gives `radiogroup` no
        sanctioned nested labelled subgroup, so the ranges/commits distinction
        would exist visually and not in the accessibility tree — the same
        failure as "colour alone", one level up. Radios also read as a form
        value, and this is a filter that takes effect immediately.

    Roving tabindex rather than `aria-activedescendant`, because Tabs and
    MenuList already do it that way; one keyboard idiom across the library beats
    a marginal difference in how one screen reader announces it. One tab stop
    for the whole picker; arrows move *and* select, because selection following
    focus is what makes a filter feel like a filter; Home and End jump to the
    ends; Enter and Space re-select the focused row; arrows wrap, as
    MenuList.Step already does.

    The zero-file scope — "Uncommitted — 0 files" — is selectable, not disabled
    and not dimmed. The count is the answer: the reader's question was "show me
    the uncommitted changes", and "there are none" answers it completely and
    often reassuringly. A disabled row puts that fact behind a wall, because
    MenuList.Step already skips disabled rows rather than focusing and refusing
    them, so the reader could not even land on it to read the count. It also
    inverts the control's meaning — disabled means *you may not*, and there is
    nothing here the reader may not do. The label stays in secondary text rather
    than dropping to --color-text-disabled, which would read as unavailable; the
    "0 files" is the whole cue, and it is a text cue.
*@

<div class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
    <ul class="change-scope__list" role="listbox" aria-label="@AriaLabel">
        @* Ranges as direct options, with no group around them. They are the
           default set and the listbox's own name already covers them; a group
           wrapper here would need an aria-label with no visible counterpart,
           which is a mismatch invented to fill a slot. *@
        @for (var position = 0; position < Scopes.Count; position++)
        {
            var index = position;
            var scope = Scopes[index];

            <li role="none">
                <button type="button"
                        class="@RowClass(scope.Id, false)"
                        role="option"
                        aria-selected="@Selected(scope.Id)"
                        tabindex="@Tabbable(index)"
                        data-testid="@RowTestId("scope", scope.Id)"
                        @ref="_elements[index]"
                        @onfocus="() => _active = index"
                        @onclick="() => SelectAsync(scope.Id)"
                        @onkeydown="args => OnKeyDownAsync(args, index)"
                        @onkeydown:preventDefault="_preventDefault">
                    @* aria-hidden because aria-selected already carries the
                       state; reading both says "checked" twice in different
                       words. The slot is always rendered, empty when unselected,
                       so selecting never shifts the labels sideways. U+2714 is
                       the glyph MarkdownView already uses for a done task — the
                       product has no icon set, and a text glyph is the standing
                       convention. *@
                    <span class="change-scope__mark" aria-hidden="true">@Mark(scope.Id)</span>
                    <span class="change-scope__label">@scope.Label</span>
                    @* "0 files" is a complete answer and stays in secondary
                       text, never disabled text — see the header. *@
                    <span class="change-scope__count">@Plural(scope.FileCount, "file")</span>
                </button>
            </li>
        }

        @if (Commits.Count > 0)
        {
            @* The divider separates a computed range from a point in history.
               Those are different kinds of thing, and a bare rule leaves the
               reader to work that out; two words of caption cost one line and
               remove the guess. The caption also names the group, so the
               accessibility tree gets the same separation the eye does. *@
            <li class="change-scope__caption" role="presentation" id="@CommitsCaptionId">@CommitsLabel</li>
            <li role="none">
                <ul class="change-scope__group" role="group" aria-labelledby="@CommitsCaptionId">
                    @for (var position = 0; position < Commits.Count; position++)
                    {
                        var index = Scopes.Count + position;
                        var commit = Commits[position];

                        <li role="none">
                            <button type="button"
                                    class="@RowClass(commit.Id, true)"
                                    role="option"
                                    aria-selected="@Selected(commit.Id)"
                                    tabindex="@Tabbable(index)"
                                    data-testid="@RowTestId("commit", commit.Id)"
                                    @ref="_elements[index]"
                                    @onfocus="() => _active = index"
                                    @onclick="() => SelectAsync(commit.Id)"
                                    @onkeydown="args => OnKeyDownAsync(args, index)"
                                    @onkeydown:preventDefault="_preventDefault">
                                <span class="change-scope__mark" aria-hidden="true">@Mark(commit.Id)</span>
                                @* Sha first because it is the row's identity,
                                   subject second because it is what a human
                                   recognises, age last because it is the
                                   tiebreaker. The accessible name is the three
                                   read in that order. *@
                                <span class="change-scope__sha">@commit.ShortSha</span>
                                <span class="change-scope__subject">@commit.Subject</span>
                                <span class="change-scope__age">@commit.Age</span>
                            </button>
                        </li>
                    }
                </ul>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
</div>

@code {
    private readonly string _id = "change-scope-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");

    private readonly List<string> _order = [];

    private ElementReference[] _elements = [];

    private int _active = -1;
    private int _focusTarget = -1;
    private bool _preventDefault;

    /// <summary>The ranges at the top of the list.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<ChangeScope> Scopes { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The commits under the divider. Empty draws neither the caption
    /// nor the group: a heading over nothing is furniture.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<ChangeCommit> Commits { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>What the divider's caption says, and what names the commit
    /// group to a screen reader.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string CommitsLabel { get; set; } = "Commits";

    /// <summary>The selected row. One id space across ranges and commits,
    /// because exactly one thing is selected across both — a picker where a
    /// range and a commit could each be "the selected one" would need the reader
    /// to hold two selections in their head to know what the panes beside it are
    /// showing. Two-way bindable, like <see cref="FolderView.SelectedPath"/>, so
    /// a host that only wants the rows to be clickable does not have to hold the
    /// selection itself.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? SelectedId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string?> SelectedIdChanged { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string AriaLabel { get; set; } = "Change scope";

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The rows as one flat sequence, which is what the keyboard walks:
    /// the divider is a visual and semantic grouping, not a boundary the arrow
    /// keys should stop at.</summary>
    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        _order.Clear();
        _order.AddRange(Scopes.Select(scope => scope.Id));
        _order.AddRange(Commits.Select(commit => commit.Id));

        if (_elements.Length != _order.Count) _elements = new ElementReference[_order.Count];
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Moving the roving tabindex is not moving focus. Without this the ring
    /// stays on the row the reader arrived at while the selection walks away
    /// from it, and the next arrow steps from the stale row rather than the
    /// visible one.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (_focusTarget < 0) return;

        var target = _focusTarget;
        _focusTarget = -1;

        if (target >= _elements.Length) return;

        try
        {
            await _elements[target].FocusAsync();
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException)
        {
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Which row Tab reaches: the one last focused, or failing that the
    /// selected one, or the first. Exactly one, which is the whole point of a
    /// roving tabindex.</summary>
    private int ActiveIndex
    {
        get
        {
            if (_active >= 0 && _active < _order.Count) return _active;

            var selected = SelectedId is null ? -1 : _order.IndexOf(SelectedId);

            return selected >= 0 ? selected : 0;
        }
    }

    private string Tabbable(int index) => index == ActiveIndex ? "0" : "-1";

    private bool IsSelected(string id) => string.Equals(SelectedId, id, StringComparison.Ordinal);

    private string Selected(string id) => IsSelected(id) ? "true" : "false";

    private string Mark(string id) => IsSelected(id) ? "✔" : string.Empty;

    /// <summary>Selection is carried by four channels, none of them colour on its
    /// own: <c>aria-selected</c>, the tick — which survives Windows High Contrast
    /// where a background tint is dropped — the border and accent strip this
    /// class brings, and the heavier label weight, which is allowed only because
    /// it is the fourth and is redundant.</summary>
    private string RowClass(string id, bool isCommit)
    {
        var kind = isCommit ? " change-scope__row--commit" : string.Empty;
        var selected = IsSelected(id) ? " change-scope__row--selected" : string.Empty;

        return $"change-scope__row{kind}{selected}";
    }

    private string? RowTestId(string kind, string id) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-{kind}-{id}";

    private string CommitsCaptionId => _id + "-commits";

    private string ClassValue => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? "change-scope" : $"change-scope {CssClass}";

    private static string Plural(int count, string noun) => $"{count} {noun}{(count == 1 ? string.Empty : "s")}";

    private async Task SelectAsync(string id)
    {
        if (IsSelected(id)) return;

        SelectedId = id;
        await SelectedIdChanged.InvokeAsync(id);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Selection follows focus. A picker whose arrows move a cursor and need a
    /// second key to commit makes the keyboard reader do two things where the
    /// mouse reader does one, and there is nothing destructive here to protect
    /// them from — changing the scope only changes what the panes beside it show.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>_preventDefault</c> is read at render time rather than from this
    /// handler, so it takes effect from the next keystroke — the same shape
    /// MenuList uses, kept identical on purpose rather than fixed here alone.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task OnKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs args, int index)
    {
        _preventDefault = args.Key is "ArrowDown" or "ArrowUp" or "Home" or "End" or " " or "Enter";

        if (args.Key is "Enter" or " ")
        {
            await SelectAsync(_order[index]);
            return;
        }

        var target = args.Key switch
        {
            "ArrowDown" => Step(index, 1),
            "ArrowUp" => Step(index, -1),
            "Home" => _order.Count == 0 ? -1 : 0,
            "End" => _order.Count - 1,
            _ => -1
        };

        if (target < 0) return;

        _active = target;
        _focusTarget = target;

        await SelectAsync(_order[target]);
    }

    /// <summary>Wraps, because a list this short has no bottom worth stopping
    /// at and a reader who overshoots should not have to travel back.</summary>
    private int Step(int from, int direction) =>
        _order.Count == 0 ? -1 : ((from + direction) % _order.Count + _order.Count) % _order.Count;
}
